# Going Beyond Prompt Libraries

Prompt libraries are a great place to start exploring the potential of Large Language Models and AI use in education. They provide ready-to-use examples that can help you see what’s possible and give you a starting point for common tasks.

However, prompt libraries are not exhaustive. Every classroom is different—your students, your content, your goals, and your context all shape the kinds of support you need. A prompt that works well in one situation may need to be adjusted, or completely rewritten, for another.

To use AI effectively in your teaching practice, it’s important to move beyond copying prompts and begin adapting and creating your own. This allows you to:
* Tailor AI responses to your specific students and learning goals
* Ask more precise and meaningful questions
* Get higher-quality, more relevant outputs

Rather than thinking of prompts as fixed templates, it’s helpful to think of prompting as a skill. Like any skill, it improves with practice.

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Prompt Engineering:</strong> A technique that involves refining and adjusting the inputs given to AI models to achieve more accurate or desired outputs.</p>
</div>

There are a number of ways to format prompts to get better outputs from LLMs. One simple prompt engineering structure you can use is **TaCo FoRT**.

